vice; no JSX, browser UI or external AI.", "Keep module paths services/graph/models.jac, services/graph/localdb.jac, services/graph/crud.jac, services/graph/actions.jac and main.jac, importing with dotted paths such as services.graph.models.", "Keep public walker names list_properties, list_units, list_tickets, list_vendors, register_tenant, db_health, list_tenants, add_property_node, delete_tenant_node, delete_property_node, match_tenant, approve_dispatch, resolve_ticket, public function check_db_health, and model names Property, Unit, Lease, Tenant, Ticket, Vendor, HasUnit, HasLease, HasTenant, HasTicket, Services, AssignedTo, EventType, UrgencyLevel."]}, "implementation_plan": "# Implementation plan

## Goal

Build the headless graph backend for Rentora, a small property-management service. In services/graph/models.jac define the graph: Property, Unit, Lease, Tenant, Ticket and Vendor nodes, the HasUnit, HasLease, HasTenant, HasTicket, Services and AssignedTo edges, and EventType and UrgencyLevel enums. In services/graph/localdb.jac keep a small local SQLite log store with a check_db_health function. In services/graph/crud.jac expose walkers that use those models: list_properties, list_units, list_tickets, list_vendors, register_tenant, db_health, list_tenants, add_property_node, delete_tenant_node, delete_property_node and match_tenant. In services/graph/actions.jac add approve_dispatch and resolve_ticket walkers that find a ticket by ticket_id under a property's units and mark it dispatched or resolved. main.jac only re-exports the crud and action walkers. An owner should be able to add a property with unit numbers, register a tenant into a unit (creating the unit and a lease if needed and refusing duplicate phones), list properties, units, tenants and tickets, match a tenant by phone, dispatch and resolve tickets, and delete properties.

